OLD/NEW PERMISSION AND GOVERNANCE CHECK:
- Old Principle I's blanket TLS prohibition is narrowed explicitly to tunneled application TLS; authenticated IPC/admin TLS is now permitted. Bounded negotiation may handle authentication/destination/isolation, but cannot inspect application bytes, infer first-party identity from application headers, or echo sensitive values. This is a semantic boundary change, expressly disclosed, with contracts and T147's implementation/refusal decision required before ingress changes.
- The old broad outbound ban is replaced by an exhaustive actor matrix. Exact internal service resolution, native sidecar maintenance and explicitly invoked isolated artifact retrieval are named permissions. None authorizes DP/CP public-network access, CP SOCKS egress, external polling/notifications, or cover traffic. T134/T138 require enforcement outside compromised CP code with packet/capability controls. No runtime updater or new route implementation is smuggled into this amendment.
- The loopback/tunnel invariant is strengthened to actual listener namespaces, publications, supported address families and lifecycle failure. T125's wildcard exception is explicitly withdrawn rather than accepted retrospectively.
- Isolation expands from first-party username sketches to device/profile/initiating-site partitions. Missing context, unsupported ingress and browser failure cannot downgrade the selected protected route. T136/T147/T157 and T126/T155 own distinct mechanisms and real-network falsifiers.
- CP-outage continuity is made explicit: valid retained authority has no outage-only expiry; undelivered revocations remain pending; accepted revocations reject new admissions while existing pipes may finish. Atomic acknowledged-state durability and stale-backup refusal strengthen recovery. This is an explicit limitation, not an authorization to call an undelivered revocation successful. See Minor m2 for its incomplete threat-model propagation.
- Completed immutable aggregate publication, operational-log restrictions and third-party diagnostic boundaries strengthen the old aggregate rule. No timestamped administrative-journal exception is accepted. Current code/schema gaps remain T152/T158/T159 work.
- Old governance contained conflicting historical remote/public-release descriptions, but its later recorded pre-release procedure expressly deferred PR/comment/version ceremony until first public release. The proposed active procedure resolves that conflict and expands mandatory impact review beyond I/III/V. The recorded maintainer request authorizes this strengthening/reconciliation; no extra weakened guarantee is accepted by this reviewer.
- The old external-human v1/major-release gate is retained and expanded to material security-boundary changes regardless of version. Actual review, candidate binding and missing evidence remain obligations; this verdict neither completes T156's delivery nor substitutes for the external review.

