Minor m2 — The required CP-outage revocation limitation has not been stated in the threat model itself.
- Where: .specify/memory/constitution.md:102-104; THREAT-MODEL.md:321.
- Affected property: Constitution II's explicit disclosure requirement and Principle V's truthful operational boundaries. The constitution states retained auth has no CP-outage-only expiry and requires the UI and threat model to disclose the consequence. THREAT-MODEL's compromised-device advice still says token revocation is the response without explaining pending delivery, the acknowledgment boundary, or surviving established pipes.
- Reachable attack path: an operator containing a stolen bearer credential during CP/DP disconnection could read that advice as immediate revocation, while the required policy permits admission under the retained generation until delivery. This is an omission in the amended document set, not evidence of a new runtime regression; the constitution and foundation contract already state the correct policy and T159 owns implementation.
- Attempted falsification: searched the complete threat model for revocation/revoked, CP outage/unreachable, pending delivery, snapshot and rollback. Its new top-level supersession notice and external foundation-contract link do not actually state this named residual exposure. Existing no-auto-expiry text concerns guest-device token lifetime, not undelivered revocation semantics.
- EVALUATED-AS: documentation propagation. Add a concise required-target/current-gap paragraph naming indefinite retained admission during outage, pending versus acknowledged revocation, existing-pipe behavior, operator stop-access action and T159/T132 ownership. Keep implementation proof pending. The UI owner must carry the same wording when that feature is implemented; this amendment need not create a UI.

VERIFIED ABSENT / CHECKLIST COVERAGE:
- DNS: no new destination-resolution permission. Read Constitution I/III, foundation deployment section and T134/T136/T147 acceptance. Internal names are exact configured identities; public UDP/DNS refusal needs packet controls. Direct adapter resolution stays at its separate boundary.
- Header/payload propagation: no permission to inspect tunneled headers/TLS. Bounded proxy negotiation and post-establishment close-only failure are explicit; CONNECT cannot waive isolation. Existing ingress repair stays owned by T147.
- Logs/metrics: no accepted per-event or changing-current-hour exception. Read amended data-model/control-grpc contracts, foundation aggregate policy, T149/T152/T158 and T155. Writers, repeated RPC reads, clock regression, retention and legacy cleanup have explicit closure probes.
- Sidecar contracts: no new sidecar invocation or mutable input is delivered. T134/T138 require independently enforced caller/port and observer permissions; future changes need reviewed contracts and positive/negative controls.
- Authentication: no loopback auth bypass or snapshot-failure success is authorized. T135/T148/T159 require both peer roles, full/resumed checks, validity/renewal, lifecycle, durable acknowledgment and stale-state rejection. T132 consumes those prerequisites.
- Binding/reachability: no wildcard or tunnel-elsewhere exception remains. Constitution Deployment Model and T134/T155 require namespace/publication, startup/restart, supported-family and ingress/egress tests; current implementation is expressly unproven/nonconforming.
- Browser APIs: no extension manifest or execution/DOM sink is added by the diff. Existing hardening API limitations remain explicit; T136/T150/T151/T157 require truthful context, UI and actual blocked-network outcomes rather than descriptor-only claims.
- Inter-plane privacy: no per-request CP gate, request-observation RPC permission or stale-event dial suppression is introduced. T073's required negative regression, T132/T159 recovery and T138 sanitized observation preserve the boundary. Wire changes require explicit transition/compatibility evidence.
- Updates: no automatic promotion, runtime polling or unpinned toolchain change is authorized. Constitution IV/VI and architecture/build pins remain binding. T140/T141/T145/T081 own unresolved integrity/release evidence; T111 must not turn documented variance into permission to omit required pinning.
- Cover/decoy traffic: no endpoint cover, speculative destination probe or circuit-preheat permission. Native sidecar protocol traffic does not transfer authority to DP/CP.
- Outbound origins: no CP-to-SOCKS or arbitrary LAN/public egress exception. The actor matrix is exhaustive, helpers are separate constrained actors, and compromised-CP enforcement is a required design property rather than an in-process allowlist claim.

LIMITS AND DISPOSITION:
- No Blocker/Major was accepted or waived. Minors do not authorize the caller to ignore the new invariants.
- Known runtime defects remain blocking remediation work. The meaningful privacy tests are still the real-namespace/browser/IPC/crash probes required by their owners and T155, followed by T078/T079 and the external release gate.
